# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Aidstack Insights — server-side AI configuration (all optional)
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. Anything set here acts as a FALLBACK: values a user
# enters in the in-app Settings modal always take precedence. Leaving this file
# empty is fine — users then supply their own credentials in the browser.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which provider to use when the browser does not specify one: openai | azure
LLM_PROVIDER=openai

# --- Option A: OpenAI ------------------------------------------------------
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini

# --- Option B: Azure AI Foundry / Azure OpenAI ------------------------------
# Set LLM_PROVIDER=azure above, then fill these in.
#
# ENDPOINT   : the base URL of your Azure resource, no trailing path.
#              Azure portal -> your resource -> "Keys and Endpoint".
# API_KEY    : either of the two keys shown on that same page.
# DEPLOYMENT : the *deployment* name you created under "Model deployments"
#              in Azure AI Foundry. This is chosen by your organization and is
#              often NOT the same as the underlying model name.
# API_VERSION: optional; defaults to 2024-10-21.
#
# AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT=https://your-resource.openai.azure.com
# AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY=your-azure-key
# AZURE_OPENAI_DEPLOYMENT=your-deployment-name
# AZURE_OPENAI_API_VERSION=2024-10-21

# --- Option C: Jev (typesafe.ai) --------------------------------------------
# Used by the "Jev Classification" mode, which returns a typed choice from a
# controlled list instead of free text. Independent of LLM_PROVIDER above:
# the mode is chosen per analysis in the UI, not by this variable.
#
# API_KEY : create one at https://console.typesafe.ai
# MODEL   : optional; defaults to jev-latest. Pin an exact version (e.g.
#           jev-1.13.0) if you tune confidence thresholds against it.
#
# TYPESAFE_API_KEY=sk-...
# TYPESAFE_MODEL=jev-latest

# --- Local server options ---------------------------------------------------
# PORT=8080
# HOST=127.0.0.1

# --- Pipeline Studio sign-in (magic link) -----------------------------------
# Setting an approved list turns sign-in ON: Pipeline Studio and /api/pipeline/*
# then require an approved, signed-in email, and the server credentials above are
# used only for signed-in users (anywhere in the app). Keys a person enters in
# AI connections still take precedence. Leave both lists empty to keep Pipeline
# Studio open and the server credentials available to every request.
#
# Comma-separated. Domains match exactly: ifrc.org does not admit sub.ifrc.org.
# AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS=someone@example.org,another@example.org
# AUTH_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=ifrc.org
#
# Signs session cookies and sign-in links. Required when sign-in is on.
# Generate with: python3 -c "import secrets;print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
# SECRET_KEY=
#
# Public address used in emailed links (avoids trusting the Host header).
# APP_BASE_URL=https://insights.aidstack.ai
#
# SMTP for sending the links: the same settings as the AI Grant Writer.
# Port 465 uses SSL; other ports use STARTTLS.
# EMAIL_SERVER_HOST=smtp.example.com
# EMAIL_SERVER_PORT=587
# EMAIL_SERVER_USER=
# EMAIL_SERVER_PASSWORD=
# Quote the whole value: EMAIL_FROM="Name <address>" (Gmail sends from the account address).
# EMAIL_FROM="Aidstack Insights <your-account@gmail.com>"
